Six p orbitals at the three 3c sites of a simple cubic cell, a pair at each
site perpendicular to the site's own axis: the orbitals at the three sites
are one another's images.

1. the model: a p pair at each 3c site, perpendicular to the site's own axis
   site              axis   orbitals
   (0, 0.5, 0.5)     x      py, pz
   (0.5, 0, 0.5)     y      px, pz
   (0.5, 0.5, 0)     z      px, py
   6 orbitals, 6 bands; 66 hoppings, random, to every orbital within
   one lattice constant, averaged over the operations of T_d

2. the group: what the point set allows, and what the hoppings obey
   the point set allows : #221.93 Pm-3m1'
   the hoppings obey    : #215.71 P-43m1', orbitals at Wyckoff 3c
   broken               : i, 3 like m(z), 6 like C2([110]), 6 like C4(z), 8 like S3([111]),
                          each also with time reversal: 48 of the 96 coset
                          representatives of the lattice translations
   about the site axes  : C4(x), C4(y), C4(z) broken; S4(x), S4(y), S4(z) kept
   -> the hoppings break inversion, and the operations lost are inversion
      times those kept: each site loses the fourfold rotation about its own
      axis and keeps the -4 rotoinversion about it

3. the atomic-limit table: what each orbital carries, read off the hoppings
orbital  Wyckoff  site symmetry  function  irrep     named by  determined by  orbital characters
      0       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(y)=0, C2(x)=-2, m([011])=0, S4(x)=0
      1       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(y)=0, C2(x)=-2, m([011])=0, S4(x)=0
      2       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(x)=0, C2(y)=-2, m([101])=0, S4(y)=0
      3       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(x)=0, C2(y)=-2, m([101])=0, S4(y)=0
      4       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(z)=-2, C2(x)=0, m([110])=0, S4(z)=0
      5       3c           -42m                E  point group    hamiltonian  E=+2, C2(z)=-2, C2(x)=0, m([110])=0, S4(z)=0
  orbitals 0-5 at 3c induce the elementary band representations of T1 at 1b (-43m) + T2 at 1b (-43m)   (.band_representations)
   -> nothing is declared: the hoppings fix each row's E themselves, the two-dimensional
      irrep of the site's own -42m, and no row names a function: which real functions
      a pair of Wannier functions is, inside its site, is a choice of basis, and
      the orbital characters are what the hoppings fix.
   each site's characters, named in its own axes:
      (0, 0.5, 0.5)   axis x:  E +2,  C2(x) -2,  C2(y) 0,  S4(x) 0,  m([011]) 0
      (0.5, 0, 0.5)   axis y:  E +2,  C2(y) -2,  C2(x) 0,  S4(y) 0,  m([101]) 0
      (0.5, 0.5, 0)   axis z:  E +2,  C2(z) -2,  C2(x) 0,  S4(z) 0,  m([110]) 0
   -> at every site the character is that of E: +2 on the identity, -2 on the twofold
      about the site's own axis, and 0 on the -4 about it, on a perpendicular twofold
      and on a diagonal mirror.  The numbers are the same at the three sites, and the
      operations they belong to are not: each is named in its own site's axes.
   -> C3([111]) carries each site onto the next, and its axis onto the next site's:
         (0, 0.5, 0.5)   -> (0.5, 0, 0.5)   :  axis x -> y,  C2(x) -> C2(y), character -2 at both
         (0.5, 0, 0.5)   -> (0.5, 0.5, 0)   :  axis y -> z,  C2(y) -> C2(z), character -2 at both
         (0.5, 0.5, 0)   -> (0, 0.5, 0.5)   :  axis z -> x,  C2(z) -> C2(x), character -2 at both
      so each site's characters are the image of the first site's, carried by the
      operations of the orbit, and each row is the E of its own site's -42m.
   -> E is named by the point group: at 3c of P-43m1' it induces a composite band
      representation, one of the known exceptions to an irrep at a maximal Wyckoff
      position inducing an elementary one, so no elementary band representation
      of the tables carries its name; the line under the table writes out the
      elementary ones it is the sum of.

4. the same model, every pair rotated inside itself by 0.37 rad
   a change of orbital basis at every site, the same at each: a choice of
   Wannier gauge
   the hoppings obey    : #215.71 P-43m1'
   its atomic-limit table is section 3's, line for line: True
   -> the group, the irreps and the characters at every row are those of section 3:
      a character is a trace, and no change of basis inside a pair moves a trace.
   the six bands        : GM4 + GM5 + M1 + M2 + 2×M5 + R4 + R5 + X1 + X2 + 2×X5
   rotated by 0.37 rad  : GM4 + GM5 + M1 + M2 + 2×M5 + R4 + R5 + X1 + X2 + 2×X5

5. the six bands as a sum of elementary band representations
   decompose            : symmetry-indicated decomposable, 1 solution
                          = 1 x T1↑G(3)@1b(-43m1',-43m) + 1 x T2↑G(3)@1b(-43m1',-43m)
   -> the band representation E at 3c induces is this sum of elementary
      ones at 1b: that is what composite means.  The line under the
      atomic-limit table of section 3 is the same sum, each term written there
      as its site irrep at its Wyckoff position.
   the figure           : the six bands along GM-X-M-GM-R-X, one spectrum in both bases,
                          max |E - E_rotated| = 0.000000 along the path

